.TH RANGE_INIT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
range_init \- Initialize a range structure

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/range.h>

.IB void
.IB range_init(range* r, void* start, size_t n_elem, size_t elem_size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBrange_init\fR function initializes a range structure with the specified start address, number of elements, and element size. It sets up the range's start and end pointers accordingly.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB r
The pointer to the range structure to be initialized.
.TP
.IB start
The starting address of the range.
.TP
.IB n_elem
The number of elements in the range.
.TP
.IB elem_size
The size of each element in the range. If zero, the size is assumed to be one.

.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.IB range_init
No return value.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBrange_init\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/range.h>

int main() {
    range r;
    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    range_init(&r, arr, 5, sizeof(int));

    // Use the range structure

    return 0;
}
.nef

.SH SEE ALSO
.IB range,
.IB range_size,
.IB range_end